# Synap Core settings. Copy to .env and fill in what you need.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Docker reads this file when it creates the server container. After you change it, run
#   docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.yml up -d
# again so the server picks the change up.
#
# Database, Redis and Neo4j passwords are NOT set here: they are generated on the first run
# and kept in a Docker volume (see deploy/docker-compose.yml). No store publishes a port.

# --- The model Synap uses ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core ships no default model: you choose, and Synap will not start until you do. One provider and
# one model are used for every step: reading your conversations, the graph, deciding whether a new
# memory replaces an old one, compaction, and describing images. Replace both placeholders.
SYNAP_LLM_PROVIDER=add-your-provider     # openai | anthropic | ollama | openai-compatible
SYNAP_LLM_MODEL=add-your-model

# The size of your model's context window, in tokens. Compaction sizes its calls from it. Synap knows the window of
# the well-known hosted models by name. For a model you run yourself, or a new one, it assumes 128000 and says so
# in the log at start: set this to the real size then. A value under 4000 is refused.
# SYNAP_LLM_CONTEXT_WINDOW=

# Optionally a different model for one step (the provider stays the same). Uncomment and fill in:
# SYNAP_LLM_MODEL_INGESTION=add-a-model
# SYNAP_LLM_MODEL_GRAPH=add-a-model
# SYNAP_LLM_MODEL_KNOWLEDGE_UPDATE=add-a-model
# SYNAP_LLM_MODEL_COMPACTION=add-a-model
# SYNAP_LLM_MODEL_VISION=add-a-model-that-can-read-images

# What your provider needs. Fill in only the lines for the one you chose.
#   openai:             OPENAI_API_KEY
#   anthropic:          ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   ollama:             a model you run yourself, outside this Compose file. Pull it first.
#   openai-compatible:  any server that speaks the OpenAI chat API (a hosted open-weights provider,
#                       vLLM, LM Studio, your company's gateway). Needs its address and, usually, a key.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
SYNAP_LLM_BASE_URL=
SYNAP_LLM_API_KEY=

# Ollama only. Where Ollama listens, as seen from the container, and how much text it may read at once.
# Ollama's own default window is small and silently cuts long prompts, so Synap asks for 16384 unless you change it.
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
# OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=16384
# OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=30m

# --- Forget by description (optional) ---------------------------------------------------------------
# "Forget" finds the memories that match a description ("forget that I live in Paris") with a Jev model
# (api.typesafe.ai). It works only with your own Jev API key; the text of the candidate memories is sent to Jev.
# Without a key the call answers that forget needs one. Deleting one memory by its id needs no key.
# Synap uses this key for forget and for nothing else.
# JEV_API_KEY=

# --- Embeddings ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "sentence-transformers" runs the model inside the server (downloaded on first start, about 2.3 GB).
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=sentence-transformers

# --- Access ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The server has no login. It listens on 127.0.0.1:8000 only. To require a token on every request,
# set one and send it as a bearer token:
# SYNAP_API_TOKEN=

# The MACA plugin (optional, needs a Synap Cloud account with credits): a Synap Cloud management key, not an
# instance API key. It is sent to Synap Cloud and nowhere else, and the server never returns it or writes it to a
# log. See the MACA plugin page for how to mint it.
# SYNAP_CLOUD_API_KEY=

# Which user the server runs as inside its container. On Linux, set this to your own user so the
# originals/ folder is writable. Compose reads this one, not the server, so it must be in your shell
# (or passed with --env-file), not only in this file:  export SYNAP_USER=$(id -u):$(id -g)
# Changing it later is fine: the downloaded models are readable by any user and are never fetched again.
# SYNAP_USER=10001:10001

# The port the server answers on, on this machine. Use it when something else already uses 8000. Like SYNAP_USER,
# Compose reads it from your shell:  export SYNAP_PORT=8010  Then point your application at the same port:
# SYNAP_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8010
# SYNAP_PORT=8000

# Copies of the text you send Synap, uploaded files and conversation backups are written to the
# originals/ folder next to this file (SYNAP_FILE_STORE=local is set in the compose file). No S3 is needed.

# The image to run. By default Compose builds it from the Dockerfile in this folder and this setting only names
# the result. To run the published image instead (no build), set these two in your shell, not in this file,
# because Compose reads them from your shell:
#   SYNAP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/maximem-ai/maximem_synap_core:latest SYNAP_PULL_POLICY=always docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.yml up -d
# SYNAP_IMAGE=synap-core:local
# SYNAP_PULL_POLICY=build

# --- Other settings (all optional; each line shows the value the server uses when you leave it out) -------------
# Remove the leading # to change one, then run the compose command above again.

# What the server writes to its log: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING or ERROR.
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# "production" turns the /docs, /redoc and /openapi.json pages off. Any other value leaves them on behind a
# login: the user is "admin" and the password is DOCS_PASSWORD. With no password those pages refuse everyone.
# ENVIRONMENT=development
# DOCS_PASSWORD=

# Browser origins allowed to call the server from another address, comma separated. The browser page at /browser
# is served by the server itself and does not need this. Without it only these two development origins are allowed.
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3001

# How many workers read each ingestion stage at once. More workers take more of your model provider's rate limit.
# STAGE1_WORKER_COUNT=1
# STAGE23_WORKER_COUNT=3
# STAGE4_WORKER_COUNT=2

# Postgres connections the server keeps: the pool size, how many more it may open under load, and how long
# a request waits for one, in seconds. DB_ECHO=true writes every SQL statement to the log.
# DB_POOL_SIZE=5
# DB_MAX_OVERFLOW=10
# DB_POOL_TIMEOUT=30
# DB_ECHO=false

# When a conversation ends, Synap compacts it. Set this to 0 to stop that.
# SYNAP_SESSION_END_COMPACTION=1

# Tracing with OpenTelemetry. Off by default; while it is off nothing is sent. When on, spans go to this OTLP address.
# SYNAP_OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=false
# SYNAP_OBSERVABILITY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4319
